# AGENTS.md — {{PROJECT_NAME}}

A static site generated from Swift with [WingedSwift](https://github.com/micheltlutz/Winged-Swift).
Read this before changing anything.

## How this project works

`swift run` executes `Sources/{{PROJECT_NAME}}/main.swift`, which builds a tree of `HTMLTag`
objects and writes HTML files into `dist/`. There is no server and no runtime: everything happens
at build time.

```
Sources/{{PROJECT_NAME}}/
├── main.swift          # the pages and what gets written where
├── SiteLayout.swift    # the shell shared by every page (head, nav, footer)
└── Components.swift    # content data + reusable markup
assets/                 # css/js/images copied into dist/
dist/                   # generated — do not edit by hand
```

## Commands

| Command | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `winged build` | Generates the site into `dist/`. |
| `winged serve --watch` | Serves `dist/` on http://localhost:8000 and rebuilds on change. |
| `swift build` | Type-checks without generating. |

## Rules

1. **Never edit `dist/`.** It is overwritten on every build. Change the Swift source.
2. **Never invent a tag type.** Check the
   [tag catalog](https://github.com/micheltlutz/Winged-Swift/blob/main/.claude/skills/wingedswift/references/tag-catalog.md);
   if an element has no type, use `HTMLTag("element-name") { … }` or `RawHTML("<svg …>")`.
3. **Content is escaped by default.** Pass raw text — never pre-escape. `escapeContent: false` is
   only for markup you generated yourself.
4. **Add a page** by creating a `Document` in `main.swift` with `SiteLayout(...).page { … }` and
   adding it to the `generateMultiple(documents:)` list.
5. **Add a section or card** as a `static func … -> HTMLTag` in `Components.swift`, then use it in
   `main.swift`. Keep the copy in `Content`, not inline in the markup.
6. **Style with classes**, chained: `.addClass("card")`, `.addClasses(["grid", "gap-4"])`. Edit the
   CSS in `assets/css/`.

## Before you say you are done

```bash
winged build
```

Then open `dist/index.html` and confirm the change is actually in the generated HTML — a build that
succeeds is not proof the page looks right.
